i have to tell you and reiterate that we  are  very concerned uh by the prc's  provocative military activity near  taiwan  tension certainly is growing between  china and taiwan and that in turn is  prompting a new debate about whether the  u.s needs a new policy approach to  prevent china from making a move on  taiwan an act that could start a wider  war in asia and between the u.s and  china specifically as we said  the activity is destabilizing  it risks miscalculation  uh and it has the potential to undermine  regional peace and stability china of  course considers taiwan a breakaway  province that should be made part of  china  taiwan  jungle  that has been china's position for a  long time though beyond rhetoric it  hasn't taken steps to actually forcibly  swallow up taiwan in the last year or so  though and particularly in the last few  months china has been acting more like a  country that might use its growing and  increasingly sophisticated military to  move on taiwan and attempt to force it  to unify china has sent repeated flights  of its military aircraft near taiwan  including many in areas taiwan considers  part of its air defense perimeter the  flights have been growing both larger in  number and more frequent  there have also been stepped up chinese  naval actions and recently satellite  photos showed china building up its  three air bases closest to taiwan  this obviously concerns taiwan but it's  also of direct concern to the u.s in  fact it's caused some analysts to argue  that it's time for the u.s to abandon  the approach it's taken in addressing  taiwanese concerns that approach is  called strategic ambiguity strategic  ambiguity means that while the u.s  provides military equipment and some  military training to taiwan it's never  said explicitly whether it would or  would not come to taiwan's defense if it  were attacked by china that's been the  case for some four decades now  the rationale behind strategic ambiguity  has been that it creates on the one hand  enough fear in china's mind that an  attack on taiwan would bring an american  military response that china will be  reluctant to move while on the other  hand not giving taiwan such a flat  assurance america would come to its  rescue that it does something too bold  and reckless and brings on the very  fight the us is trying to avoid  now though some are arguing that the  time for strategic ambiguity has ended  and that the growing threat from china  calls instead for strategic clarity that  is these voices argue the u.s should  make it clear it will come to taiwan's  defense why well the argument goes china  is a different country now than it was  four decades ago it's far stronger  militarily and much more assertive in  international affairs moreover u.s  actions in recent years in places such  as afghanistan and syria raised doubts  internationally about whether the u.s  will stick up for its friends under  those circumstances it's argued china  might become reckless and miscalculate  american resolve by thinking it could  move on taiwan without consequence and  the way to head off such chinese  thinking is to be clear not ambiguous  president biden caused a diplomatic stir  a few days ago when he suggested at a  town hall meeting that he actually is  moving away from strategic ambiguity  asked whether the us would come to  taiwan's defense if it were attacked he  said yes there's a commitment to do that  later though the white house walked back  that comment there is no change in our  policy our defense relationship with  taiwan is guided by the taiwan relations  act still china was quite unhappy with  that biden comment which just shows that  moving away from strategic ambiguity to  strategic clarity would make already  tense relations with china even worse  and some fear taiwan would think it has  a kind of blank check to be as  provocative with china as it would like  so put this debate on the growing list  of china issues the biden administration  has to tackle
 

